when it comes to audio Razer is known  for its headsets but with the release of  its Leviathan sound bar and subwoofer  combo it's taking a stab at the living  room with Dolby virtual speakers Dolby  Digital and Dolby Pro Logic 2 technology  the Leviathan simulates 5.1 surround  sound by converting analog stereo and  digital audio signals from your PC  gaming console home theater receiver  blu-ray player or mobile device  essentially anything with a  three-and-a-half millimeter audio jack  optical audio port or Bluetooth radio  will work just fine you can even connect  a portable NFC enabled device with a  quick tap on the sound bar to Razors new  sound bar is comprised of two foreign  speakers and two small tweeters the  controls on the top of the unit control  power volume input selection bluetooth  pairing and equalizer settings including  profiles for games movies and music the  unit also comes with two sets of  swappable feet for the sound bar so that  you can angle it forward or slightly  upwards depending on your needs the  Leviathans downward firing subwoofer is  technically a passive speaker but it is  boosted by the sound bars internal 30  watt amplifier it's worth noting that  the system does not come with a remote  and there's no visual reference for the  current volume level razor just started  shipping the Leviathan which sells for a  suggested retail price of $200 for your  money you might be able to get better  sounding speakers but the Leviathan is a  good looking an affordable option if  you're interested in graduating from  stereo audio from your TV and it has  Bluetooth support so you can use it with  your phone as well while it's not the  same as true surround sound the  Leviathan is definitely a step up from  traditional to speaker setups for more  gaming hardware and tech coverage stay  tuned to game tech at GameSpot calm
 

